1 NOTE {geni:about_me}   THE EARLS OF ROSS
2 CONT
2 CONT        The word, "Ross," comes from the old Celtic word, "ros," which was the name of the promontory of land between the Firth of Cromarty and the Firth of Dornoch. Thus one cannot say that Ross-shire was named for the Rosses; rat
2 CONC her the Rosses were named for Ross-shire. In ancient times, this area was called Moray, or Moray and Ross.
2 CONT        The title, Earl of Ross, was known as early as the reign of King Malcolm, IV, who succeeded to the Crown of Scotland in 1162. (History of the Clan Ross, by Alexander M. Ross; Dingwall, Scotland, 1932. Alexander M. Ross was,
2 CONC  at the time, editor of the Dingwall "North Star," a newspaper.) The title, "Earl of Ross," however, seems not to have been official, only honorary.
2 CONT        The "first" Earl of Ross was also named Malcolm, whose daughter married the Irish King Beolan of the royal house of Tara. Beolan's grandson was the founder of Clan Ross. The clan was sometimes also referred to as Clan Anria
2 CONC s, or Gille Andras, or Clan MicBille Andras (literally, "son of the follower of Saint Andrew," the patron saint of Scotland).
2 CONT        In 1214, King Alexander, II, of Scotland, led an army north to quell an uprising led by Donald McWilliam, a son of Donald Bane, a rival claimant to the throne. The chief of the Clan Ross of Moray at the time, Fearchar Mac a
2 CONC n t'sagirt, son of "The Red Priest of Applecross" and grandson of Beolan, helped Alexander to put down the rebellion and was knighted by the King. The "Chronicle of Melrose" records: "Maccintsacairt attacked them, and mightily ove
2 CONC rthrew the king's enemies; he cut off their heads, and presented them as new gifts to the new King; on the seventeenth day before the Kalends of July. And because of this, the lord king appointed him a new knight."
2 CONT        Fearchar was son of "Sagart," descended from the hereditary Abbots of Applecross(Sagart is the word for priest; his name translates as ‘Farquhar, son of the Priest'). By 1234, Sir Farquhar was formally recognized as "Earl o
2 CONC f Ross." (For more details, see Macrae's "Dingwall's Story of a Thousand Years.")
2 CONT        Sir Farquhar is said to be a heroic figure of the time, and a very religious man. He founded a number of churches and abbeys, including the Abbey at Fearn, where many, many Rosses are buried. In 1244, Sir Farquhar was part 
2 CONC of a commission to Rome, to inform the Pope that Scotland and England had made peace. Sir Farquhar died in 1252.
2 CONT
2 CONT        William, Farquhar's son, succeeded and was called the Second Earl of Ross. William received grants of land in Skye and Lewis. He was prominent in the time of King Alexander, III, and bound himself to refuse a peace with Eng
2 CONC land (the peace of his father's time having decayed). Alexander, III, had a daughter, Margaret, who was married to King Harald of Norway. William, Second Earl, swore himself to defend the claim of Alexander's daughter to the thron
2 CONC e, should Alexander not have a son. William married Jean, daughter of William Comyn, Earl of Buchan. William died in 1274.
2 CONT
2 CONT        William, II, son of William, succeeded and was called the Third Earl of Ross. He was abducted about 1250 in a revolt against the Earl's rule, but was rescued with help of the Munro family, who were rewarded with grants of l
2 CONC and and were later closely connected with the Earls of Ross. In 1263, William fought at the Battle of Largs against a Norse invasion, and supported an alliance with Llewellyn, the Welsh Prince, against the English.
2 CONT        In 1283, this William, II, supported the infant Princess Margaret, "the Maid of Norway," granddaughter of King Alexander, III, as a claimant to the throne of Scotland. He sided alternately with the English and with the Scot
2 CONC s, as did many Scottish nobles of the time, according to what they considered most advantageous at the moment to Scotland.
2 CONT        In 1291, he did homage to King Edward, I, of England, but by 1296 had withdrawn it. He led an invasion of England in 1296, which was successful for a time. However, he was captured at the Battle of Dunbar and was confined t
2 CONC o the Tower of London for seven years.
2 CONT        In 1305, he was released and appointed Warden of Spey. As Warden, he violated the religious sanctuary St. Duthac at Tain in 1306 and captured the Queen and daughter of King Robert, the Bruce; then turned them over to King E
2 CONC dward of England. In 1307, The Bruce invaded Ross-shire and Sutherland in retaliation and devastated the land. William was forced to sue for peace, at a great cost of land and revenues.
2 CONT        Afterward, William led his militia in support of The Bruce, and the two mended their quarrels. Their peace was sealed with the marriage of William's son, Hugh, to Maud, sister of Robert, The Bruce. William also had a son, J
2 CONC ohn, who married Margaret, second daughter of Alexander Comyn, fourth Earl of Buchan, and got half of her father's lands in Scotland.
2 CONT        William also had a daughter, Isabel, who married 1 June 1317, Edward Bruce, Earl of Carrick and King of Ireland, brother of Robert, The Bruce.
2 CONT        In 1312, William, II, Third Earl of Ross, supported the agreement of the Kings of Norway and Scotland, led the militia of Ross-shire at the Battle of Bannockburn, and signed the famous Declaration of Arbroath, in 1320, the 
2 CONC "Letter of Independence" of Scotland. William died at Delney, January 28, 1322.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Hugh, Fourth Earl of Ross, succeeded his father, William, II. Hugh was granted large holdings of land by the King and did other good service. He led a contingent at the Battle of Halidon Hill in 1333, where he was slain. He
2 CONC  had been married twice: first to Lady Maud Bruce, by whom he had two sons, William, III, and Hugh, II, and a daughter; he married, second, Margaret, daughter of Sir David Graham of Old Montrose, by whom he had a son, called Hugh 
2 CONC of Rariches, and a daughter, Euphemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT        William, III, eldest son of Hugh and Lady Maud Bruce, became Fifth Earl of Ross. In 1346, while taking part in one of the King's invasions of England, he assassinated Ronald of the Isles in a petty rivalry; then fled back t
2 CONC o the highlands, dreading the King's vengeance.
2 CONT        William, III, Fifth Earl, and his younger brother, Hugh, II, took part in several rebellions against the English and Scottish Kings, especially Scottish King David, II.This young Hugh, II, either died or was killed, for in 
2 CONC 1350, the Fifth Earl, with approval of his sisters and upon condition of the approval of the King, appointed his younger, half-brother, Hugh of Rariches, as his heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT        In 1372, King David, II, punished William, III, by bestowing William's daughter Euphemia's hand upon Sir Walter Leslie, and made Leslie Fourth Lord of the Isles and Sixth Earl of Ross, thus ignoring the claims of Hugh of Ra
2 CONC riches, youngest son of Hugh, Fourth Earl of Ross. Walter Leslie had a son, Alexander, Earl of Ross, and a daughter, Margaret, who married Donald, second Lord of the Isles. The first five Earls of Ross were also the first five Chi
2 CONC efs of Clan Ross, but when Sir Walter Leslie became Sixth Earl of Ross, the chiefship of the Clan passed to Hugh of Rariches.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Alexander Leslie, son of Walter Leslie, the Sixth Earl, became the Seventh Earl of Ross, and died in 1401. Alexander married a daughter of the Regent Albany; his only child, Euphemia, Countess of Ross, upon becoming a nun, 
2 CONC resigned the earldom to her uncle Sir John Stewart, Earl of Buchan and second son of Albany, who became the Eighth Earl of Ross.
2 CONT        The rightful heir, Donald, Second Lord of the Isles, who had married Lady Margaret Leslie, sister of the late Earl, waged a war for possession of the land. After several years and some successes, Donald was defeated in 1411
2 CONC  and forced to resign his pretensions. Sir John Stewart, retained the estate and the titles, Earl of Buchan and Earl of Ross, until he was slain at Verneuil in France in 1424, after which the title and the land fell to the Crown.
2 CONT        King James, I, of Scotland retained the Earldom in his own hands for some years. In 1427, hoping to put an end to the squabbling, King James invited Alexander, Third Lord of the Isles, who had succeeded his father, Donald, 
2 CONC in 1420, and his mother, Margaret, the Countess of Ross, to a meeting to settle differences. Alexander and the Countess were seized and put in prison.
2 CONT        Alexander was soon liberated and set about to make war on King James of Scotland. The King defeated Alexander several times and laid waste to Ross and the Isles in 1429. On August 27, 1429, Alexander, clad in only his under
2 CONC wear, presented himself to King James and surrendered his sword. He was imprisoned for a time.
2 CONT        In 1430, Alexander and his mother, the Countess, were pardoned and their lands and title restored. Thus Alexander became the Ninth Earl of Ross. He died at Dingwall, the Ross Castle, on May 4, 1448.
2 CONT
2 CONT        John, son of Alexander, became the Tenth Earl of Ross and the last Lord of the Isles. John led a turbulent life, first on one side, then on another. All his sons, his bastards, his half-brothers were eventually wiped out. T
2 CONC he title and the estate went back to the King of Scotland for eternity. King James, III, created a Dukedom of Ross for his son, James, IV, for a while, which went some years later to Alexander, posthumous son of King James, IV. Th
2 CONC us the title and lands of Ross went into decay. Genealogically, none of these Earls of Ross carried the surname Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT        THE ROSSES OF Balnagowan
2 CONT
2 CONT        Hugh Ross of Rariches was the first man to use Ross as his surname in 1357 and became sixth chief of Clan Ross in 1372, when his half-brother, William, III, Fifth Earl of Ross died. William, III, had named Hugh of Rariches 
2 CONC as his heir, but Hugh was prevented from ever assuming that heritage. Hugh of Rariches was son of Hugh, Fourth Earl of Ross, and his second wife, Margaret, daughter of Sir David Graham. Hugh inherited from his father land in Raric
2 CONC hes and Colluigh.
2 CONT        Hugh obtained a charter to the lands of Balnagowan in 1374, and styled himself "First Laird of Balnagowan Castle." This Hugh Ross married Margaret Barclay and had two children, William Ross, second Laird, and Jean Ross, who
2 CONC  married Robert Munro, Eighth Baron of Foulis.
2 CONT
2 CONT        William Ross, Second Laird of Balnagowan and Seventh Chief of Clan Ross, married Christina, daughter of Lord Livingstone, who was credited with building the Kirk (church) of Alness (per Reid's "Earls of Ross").
2 CONT
2 CONT        Walter Ross, Third Laird of Balnagowan and eighth Chief of Clan Ross, married Catherine, daughter of Paul Mactyre, a freebooter (pirate) who claimed to be the grandson of a Norwegian King, who had invaded Scotland earlier. 
2 CONC Paul claimed for a time to be heir to the Earldom of Ross, as a true male heir of the Fourth Earl of Ross, but his claim was ultimately discredited.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Hugh Ross, Fourth Laird of Balnagowan and Ninth Chief of Clan Ross, is said to have married a daughter of the Earl of Sutherland, but the pedigree of the House of Sutherland does not bear this out. He had three sons:
2 CONT        1. John Ross, who became Fifth Laird of Balnagowan and tenth chief of Clan Ross;
2 CONT        2. William Ross, of Little Allan, who became First Laird of Shandwick; his descendants regained the Ross arms and chiefship in 1968.
2 CONT        3. Thomas Ross, who became sub-dean of Ross and Rector of the Collegiate Church in Tain.
2 CONT
2 CONT        John Ross, Fifth Laird of Balnagowan, married Christina, daughter of Torquil Macleon of the Lewis. Their children included: 1. Alexander Ross, who became Sixth Laird of Balnagowan; 2. Donald Ross, who became first Laird of 
2 CONC Priesthill and Dean of Caithness; x. and three other sons, including one who founded the "Rosses of Balblair."
2 CONT        Two descendants of Balbair made history in America: Col. George Ross was an influential officer and a signer of the Declaration of Independence. His brother, John Ross, was killed in the Revolution; General George Washingto
2 CONC n asked John's widow, Betsy Ross, to make the first American flag.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Alexander Ross, Sixth Laird of Balnagowan, married Dorothy, daughter of Alexander Sutherland of Duffus. They had two children: 1. David Ross, who became Seventh Laird of Balnagowan; and 2. Isobell, who became the first wife
2 CONC  of George Munro, Tenth Laird of Foulis.
2 CONT        John, Earl of Sutherland, led a bloody battle of revenge against this Alexander Ross in 1486-87. After much fighting, Alexander Ross and seventeen other landed gentlemen, including two William Rosses, were slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT        David Ross, Seventh Laird of Balnagowan and twelfth chief of Clan Ross, married, first, Helen Keith, daughter of the Laird of Inverguie, by whom he had a family: 1. Walter Ross, who became Eighth Laird of Balnagowan; 2. Wil
2 CONC liam Ross, who became first Laird of Invercharron; 3. Hugh Ross, who became First Laird of Tolly and Achnacloich; and 4. Angus Ross, who married a daughter of William MacCulloch of Plaids.
2 CONT        David, Seventh Laird, married, second, a daughter of the Duke of Albany, through whose patronage the Laird was made a knight: Sir David Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Walter Ross, Eighth Laird of Balnagowan and thirteenth chief, married Marian, daughter of Sir John James Grant of Grant and had children: 1. Alexander Ross, Ninth Laird; 2. Katherine Ross, who married John Denoon of Cadgoll
2 CONC , one of the magistrates of Tain; and 3. Janet Ross, who married the Fifth Lord Lovat.
2 CONT        Walter Ross was slain in battle at Tain in 1528.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Alexander Ross, Ninth Laird of Balnagowan, married, first, Janet, daughter of John, Third Earl of Caithness, by whom he had 1. Robert Ross; 2. Hector Ross; 3. George Ross, who became Tenth Laird; 4. Catherine Ross; 5. Agnes
2 CONC  Ross, who married Duncan Campbell of Boath; and 6. Christina Ross, who married Kenneth MacKenzie, third Laird of Dochmalnuack.
2 CONT        Alexander Ross, Ninth Laird, married, second, Catherine, daughter of Kenneth MacKenzie of Kintail, and had children: 7. Nicholas Ross, who became First Laird of Pitcalnie; and 8. Malcolm, who died without issue.
2 CONT        This Alexander Ross was an avowed opponent of the Scottish King, James VI, and was imprisoned for some years, after which he was liberated. He died in 1592 and is buried with his kith and kin at Fearn, the church which Malc
2 CONC olm, the "second" Earl of Ross had founded. His daughter, Catherine, was accused of witchcraft (see Chamber's Domestic Annals of Scotland, for details) but was acquitted through intimidation of the jury.
2 CONT
2 CONT        George Ross, Tenth Laird of Balnagowan and fifteenth chief, succeeded to the land in May, 1560, and married twice: first to Marion, daughter of Sir John Campbell, or Calder, by whom he had 1. David Ross, who became Eleventh
2 CONC  Laird of Balnagowan; x. and four daughters.
2 CONT        George Ross married, second, Isobell, second daughter of Angus Mackintosh of Mackintosh, by whom he had a son, 6. Alexander Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT        David Ross, Eleventh Laird of Balnagowan and sixteenth chief, married, first, Lady Mary Gordon, who died without children; and, second, Lady Annabella Murray, by whom he had only one child: David Ross, who became the Twelft
2 CONC h Laird of Balnagowan. David Ross, Eleventh Laird, died November 20, 1632, and is buried in the family vault in the Abbey of Fearn.
2 CONT
2 CONT        David Ross, Twelfth Laird of Balnagowan, married Marie, eldest daughter of Hugh, Lord Fraser of Lovat, and had children: 1. David Ross, who became Thirteenth Laird; 2. Alexander Ross, who died at age 20; 3. Isobell Ross, wh
2 CONC o married James Innes of Lightness; and 4. Catherine Ross, who married John Mackenzie.
2 CONT        David Ross, Twelfth Laird, raised a regiment of this clansmen and went with the Scots army into England in 1643 and again in 1651, in an attempt to reinstate Charles, I, the deposed King of England and Scotland. Oliver Crom
2 CONC well defeated the Scots badly at the Battle of Worcester, slaying more than 2000 of the Scots Royalists and capturing 8000 prisoners, many of whom were deported as indentured servants to America. David escaped to France, but was s
2 CONC oon captured and imprisoned in the Tower of London for several years, where he died Dec 29, 1653. He was buried in Westminister Abbey. When the monarchy was restored in 1660, King Charles, II, settled a pension of David's heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT        David Ross, Thirteenth Laird of Balnagowan and eighteenth Chief of Clan Ross, assumed the title when he was only nine years old. He mismanaged the estate and allowed it to become seriously encumbered with debt. The death of
2 CONC  his father and several other factors got the young laird into more and more troubles. Being without issue, he contracted for 10,000 pounds to convey the land to a Mr. Francis Stewart, provided Francis would assume the Ross surnam
2 CONC e and title of Laird of Balnagowan. This David Ross died in 1711.
2 CONT        When Francis Stewart became of age and looked at the mess, he resigned his interest, it being all debt, and the land went to the creditors, William, Lord Ross, among others. As early as 1638, a William, Lord Ross, had lent 
2 CONC the family money in mortgage
2 CONT        In 1711, Lord Ross and his son, George Ross, with the consent of the Laird of Balnagowan, conveyed the estate to Lieutenant General Charles Ross, a brother of Lord Ross of Hawkhead. These Rosses were low-landers and derived
2 CONC  from the deRoos family of Normandy. Charles Ross had no children; so he left the estate in 1732 to his grand-nephew, Captain Charles Ross.
2 CONT        Thus, the estate was conveyed to another family, which had no connection with the old. Despite this, the new Lords Ross persuaded the Lord Lyon King of Arms to grant them the right to use the coat of arms of Clan Ross. Thus
2 CONC  for almost 200 years, the lands, title, and arms of Clan Ross were held by a family unrelated to the ancient Earls of Ross.
2 CONT        This Charles Ross, son of William Ross, twelfth Lord Ross, had no family of his own. He conveyed the estate to his sister's children. His sister, Grizel Ross, had married Sir James Lockhart, whose fifth son inherited the es
2 CONC tate and added the name Ross to his own. His descendants are still in possession of the Balnagowan estate, viz:
2 CONT        Sir James Lockhart-Ross, b. November 22, 1721, who became an admiral;
2 CONT        Sir Charles Lockhart-Ross, who became a Lieutenant General and died in 1814;
2 CONT        Sir Charles William Augustus Lockhart-Ross, b. 1812;
2 CONT        Sir Charles Henry Augustus Frederick Lockhart-Ross, b. 1872, inventor and manufacturer of the "Ross Rifle."
2 CONT
2 CONT        Thus, anyone is in trouble who claims direct descent from either the Earls of Ross or the Rosses of Balnagowan. Of course, there were many minor lines, many branches which could have descendants. If anyone has information o
2 CONC n any of those branches, we "twigs" would be very interested in receiving copies and documentation.
2 CONT
2 CONT        THE ROSSES OF PITCALNIE
2 CONT
2 CONT        When David Ross, thirteen Laird of Balnagowan and eighteenth Chief of the Clan Ross died in 1711 without a direct heir on the Balnagowan side, the leadership of the Clan Ross passed to Malcolm Ross, Fifth Laird of Pitcalnie
2 CONC .
2 CONT        The First Laird of Pitcalnie, b. late 1500s, d. 1611, was Nicholas Ross, second son of Alexander, Ninth Laird of Balnagowan, and his wife, Catherine MacKenzie. Nicholas married Margaret Monro, d/o Hector Monro, of Assynt.
2 CONT        David Ross, (s/o Nicholas Ross and Margaret Munro), b. ?, d. 14 Oct 1646 in Fearn, was Second Laird of Pitcalnie. He married Jean Dunbar, daughter of Alexander Dunbar.
2 CONT        Malcolm Ross, of Kindeace, (s/o David Ross and Jean Dunbar), b. ?, d. 1695; m. Jean McCulloch, d/o Thomas McCulloch, who was Provost of Tain. I don't know whether or not he became Laird of Pitcalnie.
2 CONT        Niclas Ross, (s/o Malcolm Ross of Kindeace and Jean McCulloch) immigrated to Sweden, where he was involved with an international community. He was born 1667, Scotland, d. 1706, Norrköping, Sweden. He married Charlotta de Go
2 CONC msez, b. 1680, possibly in Amsterdam, d. c1747 in Norrköping, Sweden. Her father, Tobias de Gomsez, was born 1641 in Amsterdam and died 1704 in Norrköping, Sweden. Tobias's wife was Elisabeth Limbinon (c1640-1712), d/o Pieter Lamb
2 CONC inon, who d. 22 Feb 1674 in Norrköping, Sweden, and Susanna de Fineman, b. 1613, France, d. 1693. Niclas Ross and Charlotta had at least one child: Maria Elizabet Ross, b. 1703, d. 1738.
2 CONT        ?? Third and Fourth Lairds of Pitcalnie??
2 CONT        [Can someone fill this gap?]
2 CONT
2 CONT        Malcolm Ross, Fifth Laird of Pitcalnie, reclaimed the chiefship of Clan Ross in 1711, when David Ross, thirteenth Laird of Balnagowan, died without issue.
2 CONT        While most of Clan Ross took little or no part in the Jacobite Rebellions of 1689, 1715, and 1745, the Rosses of Pitcalnie supported the Jacobite cause in 1715 against the Hanoverians, when Prince Charles Edward Stuart, The
2 CONC  Old Pretender, tried unsuccessfully to reclaim the throne of England and Scotland for the Stuarts.
2 CONT        In the early 1700s, the English decided the clan system undermined their authority and issued the "Disarming Acts" in 1716. The clans had to hand in their arms. After 1745, the clan system, including the wearing of highland
2 CONC  dress and tartans, was forbidden. But, of course, neither disappeared. When the clan system re-emerged, it was never quite the same again.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Alexander Ross, Sixth Laird of Pitcalnie, married three times. By either his first or second marriage, he had a son, Malcolm, who was replaced as Chief of Clan Ross by a younger, half-brother, Munro Ross, s/o Alexander Ross
2 CONC  and his third wife, Naiomi Ross. Munro Ross joined the regiment of the Earl of Cromarty in the Third Jacobite Rebellion, 1745, in behalf of "Bonny Prince Charlie."
2 CONT
2 CONT        Munro Ross, upon attaining his majority, became Seventh Laird of Pitcalnie, basing his claim to the Earldom of Ross in 1778 on the grounds that he was the lineal descendant of Hugh Ross, of Rarichies, the brother of the las
2 CONC t O'Beolan Earl of Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT        Captain James Ross, a cousin, became Eighth Laird of Pitcalnie on 12 June 1818. He had at least three children, who figure in the descent: 1. James Ross, Ninth Laird of Pitcalnie, from 1821 to 1829; 2. George Ross, Tenth La
2 CONC ird of Pitcalnie; An obituary notice in August 1884 records his death: "Mr. George Ross of Pitcalnie, in Ross-shire, and Arnto in Kincardine, aged 81. Deceased was the last representative of the ancient Earls of Ross, and was chie
2 CONC f of the Clan Ross." He was succeeded by a (?grand)-nephew, whose daughter, Sarah, regained the arms and the chiefship in 1903. 3. Sarah Ross, who married in 1834, Donald Williamson. The line continued through their son, John Hugh
2 CONC  Ross, who married Fanny Georgina Clarke.
2 CONT
2 CONT        John Hugh Ross and Fanny Clarke had at least four children: (since their grandmother, Sarah Ross, was heiress to both the lines of Pitcalnie and Ross, it was legal and customary for the children to take both surnames.)
2 CONT        1. George Ross Williamson-Ross, Eleventh Laird of Pitcalnie;
2 CONT        2. Ethel Frances Sarah Williamson-Ross, unmarried Chief of Clan Ross as "Ross of Pitcalnie," successfully reclaimed the arms and title of the old Earls of Ross in 1903 and lived until 1957;
2 CONT        3. Rosa Ross Williamson-Ross, was designated twenty-eighth Chief of Clan Ross, from 1957 until her death in 1968. The chiefship then passed to David Campbell Ross, son of Sir Charles Campbell Ross, of the Shandwick branch o
2 CONC f Clan Ross. David Campbell Ross, b. 11 Feb 1934, became the twenty-ninth Chief of Clan Ross. The chief's grandfather, Sir Ronald Ross of Shandwick, was awarded the Nobel Prize for medicine in 1902, for discovering the cause of ma
2 CONC laria.
2 CONT        4. Catherine Evelyn Williamson-Ross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Brashear February 28, 2005 at 07:22:28.. .
